\subsection{Exact spectral evaluation and paired validation of the
reduced physical-MSE law}
\label{sec:adaptation-reduced-mse-validation}

Section~\ref{sec:adaptation-reduced-mse-envelope} derives the reduced
physical-MSE schedule of Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-reduced-mse-law} and identifies a
direct paired comparison against the legacy amplitude-coupled cubic comparator,
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-reduced-direct-comparison}, as the appropriate empirical
test. This subsection reports that test. It first replaces the low-frequency
asymptote used in Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-reduced-physical-mse} with an exact
spectral evaluation, which also fixes the schedule coefficient analytically,
and then measures the resulting law against the legacy cubic comparator under the
paired ten-seed protocol.

\subsubsection{Exact reconstruction integral}

The asymptote $|G(j\omega)-1|^2\simeq4(\omega_R/\omega)^4$ used to obtain
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-reduced-physical-mse} is valid only for
$\omega\gg\omega_R$. Retaining the exact reconstruction error of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-G-error} gives the wave-distortion cost without that
restriction,
\begin{equation}
J_{\rm wave}(\omega_R)
=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
\frac{1+4x^2}{1+x^6}\,S_\eta^{(2)}(\omega)\,d\omega,
\qquad x=\frac{\omega}{\omega_R},
\label{eq:adapt-exact-wave-cost}
\end{equation}
so that $J=3q_{\rm eff}/(2\omega_R^3)+J_{\rm wave}$. Writing
$\kappa_\star=F(\rho)$ with $\rho=q_{\rm eff}/(\sigma_a^2\tau)$ and
$s=\partial\log\kappa_\star/\partial\log\rho$, the local exponents of
$r_S^\star=\sqrt{q_{\rm eff}/c_T}\,\tau^{5/2}\kappa_\star^{-3}$ are
\begin{equation}
p_a=6s,
\qquad
p_\tau=\tfrac{5}{2}+3s.
\label{eq:adapt-exponents-from-s}
\end{equation}
The asymptotic branch corresponds to $s=1/7$ and reproduces
$(p_a,p_\tau)=(6/7,41/14)$; an exponent $p_a=1$ would require $s=1/6$.

Equation~\eqref{eq:adapt-exact-wave-cost} was evaluated on the eight measured
directional spectra of the reference records, summed over direction, and $J$
minimized numerically in $\log\omega_R$. The results are collected in
Table~\ref{tab:adapt-exact-mse-exponents}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Exact minimization of the reduced physical MSE on the measured
reference spectra.}
\label{tab:adapt-exact-mse-exponents}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Spectrum & $H_s$ (m) & $\kappa_\star$ & $p_a$ & $p_\tau$ &
$J_{\rm wave}/J$ \\
\midrule
JONSWAP    & 0.27 & 0.556 & 0.853 & 2.927 & 0.427 \\
JONSWAP    & 1.50 & 0.403 & 0.854 & 2.927 & 0.428 \\
JONSWAP    & 4.00 & 0.399 & 0.855 & 2.927 & 0.428 \\
JONSWAP    & 8.50 & 0.320 & 0.855 & 2.928 & 0.428 \\
PM--Stokes & 0.27 & 0.513 & 0.853 & 2.926 & 0.427 \\
PM--Stokes & 1.50 & 0.382 & 0.854 & 2.927 & 0.428 \\
PM--Stokes & 4.00 & 0.370 & 0.855 & 2.927 & 0.428 \\
PM--Stokes & 8.50 & 0.315 & 0.855 & 2.928 & 0.428 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Three observations follow. First, the exact exponents reproduce the asymptotic
pair to three significant figures over the whole calibrated envelope, so
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-reduced-mse-law} is not merely a limiting form here. In
particular $p_a$ does not approach unity at the calibrated operating points.
Second, the ratio $J_{\rm wave}/J=0.428$ is constant and equals $3/7$, which is
the exact stationary value of $Bw^4/(Aw^{-3}+Bw^4)$; the minimization is
therefore located in the $\omega_R^4$ branch of the distortion cost, which is
why no departure from the asymptote is available. Third, $\kappa_\star$ is not
constant: it decreases monotonically from $0.556$ to $0.315$ across the
envelope and brackets the fixed reference $\kappa=0.3627$ of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-CR-reference-value}. The pole-placement law holds fixed a
quantity that the physical-MSE criterion requires to vary with sea state, and
that difference is the entire content of the comparison.

As an independent check, $m_{-4}$ recovered from the optimality balance
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-reduced-mse-pole} agrees with direct numerical integration
of $\omega^{-4}S_\eta^{(2)}$ to within $1$--$2\,\%$ on all eight records.

\subsubsection{Analytical schedule coefficient}

Because Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-exact-wave-cost} is evaluated rather than assumed,
the schedule coefficient is determined rather than fitted. Writing the deployed SpectralMSE
form as
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
r_{S,\rm MSE}^\star
=C_J\,q_{\rm eff}^{1/14}\,
\widehat\sigma_{a,B}^{\,6/7}\,\tau^{24/7}\,T_S^{-1/2},}
\label{eq:adapt-mse-applied-form}
\end{equation}
the coefficient $C_J$ absorbs
$\left(\int x^{-8}\Phi_a(x)\,dx\right)^{3/7}$. Solving the exact balance on the
eight reference spectra gives
\begin{equation}
\boxed{C_J\simeq0.0538.}
\label{eq:adapt-mse-coefficient}
\end{equation}
The physical band-limited amplitude $\widehat\sigma_{a,B}$ appears in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-mse-applied-form} rather than the OU prior scale
$\sigma_{aw}$, because the distortion penalty is a property of the sea and not
of the prior; the implementation divides $c_\sigma$ back out, so $C_J$ and
$c_\sigma$ are separately identifiable.

\subsubsection{Relation to the empirically calibrated coefficients}

Two analytical coefficients can now be compared with the values that were
previously obtained by calibration, and the comparison is summarized in
Table~\ref{tab:adapt-coefficient-predictions}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Analytically predicted coefficients against calibration.}
\label{tab:adapt-coefficient-predictions}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}lllr@{}}
\toprule
Coefficient & Predicted & Swept optimum & Ratio \\
\midrule
$C_R$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-cR-kappa}      & $17.11$  & $17.11$  & $1.00$ \\
$C_J$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-mse-coefficient} & $0.0538$ & $0.0538$ & $1.00$ \\
$c_\sigma$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-csigma-prediction} & $1.80$ & $\approx0.6$ & $0.33$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The two regularizer coefficients are recovered by the sweeps to the resolution
of the grids. For $C_R$ the coarse sweep minimum lies at the predicted value in
every $c_\sigma$ row over a range of a factor of sixteen; for $C_J$ the
$32$-point sweep minimum coincides with
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-mse-coefficient}. The amplitude coefficient $c_\sigma$ is
the exception, and it is also the coefficient the endpoint is least sensitive
to: once decoupled from the pseudo-measurement, its full sweep moves the
primary endpoint by only about $2$--$3\,\%$, so the disagreement with the
band-matching reference is not resolved by these data in either direction.

The predicted coefficients also carry a consistent statement about the legacy
gain. Evaluated at the nominal $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$ anchor, the schedule of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-mse-applied-form} is equivalent to a cubic coefficient of
$0.277$ in the legacy cubic parameterization $c_R\sigma_{aw}\tau^3$, against the
calibrated $c_R=0.35$. An independent gain-optimized scan at fixed exponent
$p_a=1$ selects $0.311$. Both indicate that the calibrated schedule is
approximately $10$--$25\,\%$ stronger at the anchor than either criterion
prefers, and the measured per-record improvements reported below are
concentrated in the low sea states where over-regularization is most costly.

Finally, the exponent comparison of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-calibrated-ratio-tau} explains a previously puzzling
empirical result. A scan that holds the period exponent at $5/2$ and fits only
the amplitude exponent returns $p_a\simeq1.3$, which is inconsistent with both
the legacy cubic $p_a=1$ and the predicted $p_a=6/7$. Along the calibrated
trajectory the two variables are strongly collinear, so a law is characterized
by its effective period exponent $p_a\,d\log\sigma_{aw}/d\log\tau+p_\tau$. Using
the descriptive fit of Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-calibrated-sigma-tau-fit}, the
legacy cubic law gives $3.61$ and Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-reduced-mse-law} gives $3.88$;
a $\tau^{5/2}$ parameterization can only reach the latter by inflating $p_a$ to
approximately $1.26$. The measured $p_a\simeq1.3$ is therefore the reduced-MSE
schedule observed through a parameterization that cannot represent its period
exponent, rather than evidence for a super-linear amplitude dependence.

\subsubsection{Paired ten-seed comparison}

Equation~\eqref{eq:adapt-mse-applied-form} was implemented in the estimator and
compared against the legacy cubic comparator under the paired protocol of
Sec.~\ref{sec:sim-and-eval}: identical scenarios, identical wave-phase,
IMU-noise, and initialization seeds, $n=90$ paired
$(\text{scenario},\text{repetition})$ observations over nine scenarios. Both
arms use $c_\sigma=0.9$; the reduced-MSE arm uses
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-mse-coefficient} without further adjustment.
Table~\ref{tab:adapt-mse-paired} reports the vertical-displacement endpoint.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Paired comparison of the reduced physical-MSE schedule against the
legacy cubic comparator. Vertical-displacement RMS error in percent of $H_s$ over the
final \SI{900}{s}; $n=90$ paired seed triplets.}
\label{tab:adapt-mse-paired}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Scenario & Cubic & MSE & $\Delta$ & 95\,\% h.w. \\
\midrule
Transition $H_s\,1.5\!\to\!4.0$ & 4.663 & 4.645 & $-0.018$ & 0.009 \\
JONSWAP $H_s=0.27$    & 5.128 & 4.996 & $-0.132$ & 0.043 \\
JONSWAP $H_s=1.50$    & 4.907 & 4.855 & $-0.052$ & 0.034 \\
JONSWAP $H_s=4.00$    & 4.852 & 4.866 & $+0.014$ & 0.015 \\
JONSWAP $H_s=8.50$    & 4.030 & 4.030 & $-0.000$ & 0.006 \\
PM--Stokes $H_s=0.27$ & 4.735 & 4.649 & $-0.086$ & 0.047 \\
PM--Stokes $H_s=1.50$ & 4.223 & 4.223 & $-0.001$ & 0.030 \\
PM--Stokes $H_s=4.00$ & 4.018 & 4.036 & $+0.018$ & 0.020 \\
PM--Stokes $H_s=8.50$ & 4.018 & 4.037 & $+0.019$ & 0.014 \\
\midrule
Pooled                & 4.508 & 4.482 & $\mathbf{-0.026}$ & 0.014 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The pooled paired difference is
$-0.026\,\%H_s$ with a $95\,\%$ half-width of $0.014$, a reduction of
$0.58\,\%$ relative. Four scenarios improve by more than their own interval,
one degrades by more than its own interval, and four are statistical ties. The
non-stationary transition improves rather than degrading, so the gain is not
purchased against transient response. Secondary metrics move consistently or
not at all: three-dimensional
displacement RMS improves by $\SI{0.008}{m}$ ($95\,\%$ h.w.\
$\SI{0.0015}{m}$), roll by $0.0003^\circ$, and pitch is unchanged within
$0.0002^\circ$.

Because several sub-percent effects encountered during this investigation did
not survive replication, the primary endpoint is reported with the full
companion inference of Sec.~\ref{sec:sim-and-eval}: a Student-$t$
statistic of $-3.76$ ($p=1.7\times10^{-4}$), a percentile bootstrap $95\,\%$
interval of $[-0.040,-0.013]$ excluding zero, an exact paired randomization
test with $p=2\times10^{-4}$, and an exact sign test with $52/90$ negative
differences ($p=0.17$). The sign test is the least powerful of the four for an
effect that is small relative to the per-seed spread, and the three
magnitude-sensitive statements agree. In addition, all eight deterministic
record gates pass at their unchanged thresholds, which are fitted to the
legacy cubic schedule's own worst values.

The measured outcome is therefore not the statistical tie anticipated in
Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation-reduced-mse-envelope}, but a small systematic
improvement in the predicted direction, obtained with an analytically
determined coefficient. The residual is a legitimate target for the full
$21$-state physical-$H_2$ model of Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation-full-state-h2}.

\subsubsection{Deployment configurations}

SpectralMSE is the deployed/default configuration; the cubic family is retained as a supported low-cost comparator/fallback. Over the calibrated
envelope their ratio varies only as $(\tau^3/\sigma_{aw})^{1/7}$,
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-reduced-vs-applied-ratio}, which spans approximately a
factor of $1.3$ from $H_s=\SI{0.27}{m}$ to $H_s=\SI{8.5}{m}$. Evaluating
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-mse-applied-form} requires one transcendental call per
tuner update after the constant factor $q_{\rm eff}^{1/14}$ is cached and the
product form $\widehat\sigma_{a,B}^{\,6/7}\tau^{24/7}=
(\widehat\sigma_{a,B}\tau^4)^{6/7}$ is used, against none for the cubic
comparator; the measured evaluation cost is \SI{19.8}{ns} versus \SI{3.2}{ns} per call
on a desktop target, and the disparity is considerably larger on
microcontrollers without hardware transcendental support. The cubic
schedule is therefore retained as an explicitly selectable low-cost configuration, at a documented cost of well under $1\,\%$ of the vertical
endpoint, while Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-mse-applied-form} is the deployed/default law.
